===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|armsful|armsfuls}}

# The [[amount#Noun|amount]] an [[arm#Noun|arm]] or arms can [[hold#Verb|hold]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[Joseph Blagrave]|authorlink=Joseph Blagrave|chapter=How to Order an Orchard that It shall Never Misse Bearing|title=New Additions to the Art of Husbandry. [...]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Benjamin Billingsley,{{nb...|and are to be sold at the sign of the Printing-Presse in Broad-street.}}|year=1670|page=45|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=wxR2Y_fgq-UC&pg=PA45|oclc=35245904|passage=[T]ake three or four good arms full of muckle Straw, Hay, or Fern, not too wet, not to dry, and obſerving which ſide of the Orchard the Wind blows on, then laying a good '''armful''' of muckle in three or four places according to the bigneſs of your Orchard, then get ſome dry ſticks, and having kindled them put an '''armful''' of muckle upon the Fire, and it will ſmoak and [[smother|ſmoother]], and the wind will drive the ſmoak through the whole Orchard, continue it till the wind turn out of the Eaſterly quarter, and it will preſerve the Trees and Fruit from [[blights|blites]] and all manner of flys and caterpillers, which thoſe ſorts of bliting winds uſually bring; [...]}}
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|fablenumber=CCLXXXI|fable=A Sick Hermit|page=245|passage=Upon this, by Conſent, the Doctors put a Good '''Armful''' of Warm Womans Fleſh into the Bed to him, [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=R[ichard] B[ellings]|authorlink=Richard Bellings|chapter=A Sixth Book to [[w:The Countess of Pembroke's Arcadia|The Countess of Pembroke’s Arcadia]]|mainauthor={{w|Philip Sidney}}|title=The Works of the Honourable Sir Philip Sidney, Kt.|location=London|publisher=[s.n.]|year=1724|volume=III|page=53|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KsUIAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA53|oclc=663665444|passage=[T]hough ''Pyrocles'' and ''Muſidorus'' at other times would diſpence with the length of the ſports, yet now, in reſpect of the '''armfuls''' of joys they were to expect in bed, they thought them tedious; [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Dictionaire Oeconomique|entry=PLOVER|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=k9A_AQAAMAAJ&pg=PP252|column=2|passage=The Lodge is to be made after this Manner; [...] You muſt lay a good Handful of Straw under the Cord upon the Turf, to prevent dirting the Cord, as well as ſpoiling the Turf, and you had need to have a good '''Armful''' in your Lodge, to keep you warm and dry, as Occaſion requires; [...]}}

